  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Magician's Hat",
    "overview": "One hat, endless tricks. Every page is the same structure — the same token tiers, components, spacing and motion — and the visitor reaches in and pulls out a different world: the warm, bookish **Default**; the strong, technical, precise **Blueprint**; the polished but friendly **Opal**. The magic is that nothing underneath changes. Swapping `data-preset` and `data-theme` (ADR 0004) re-dresses the whole site in one move, and the reveal is animated so the trick lands.\n\nEach preset is a complete world in its own right, not a skin over Default. Presets are partial, override-only layers in the token build, but what they override — colour, type families, case, radius, depth, backdrop, entrance distance — is chosen so each one has a distinct personality. Everything should feel fun, a little magical, and worth poking: components spring, nudge and press back.\n\nDensity is relaxed. Content sits in a prose measure (68ch) or a 12-column grid with a fluid gutter, with plenty of air above and between blocks. Type is fluid (`clamp()` everywhere) rather than stepping at breakpoints (ADR 0007).\n\n**Rejected:** the generic SaaS/template look — centred hero, three feature cards, gradient call-to-action. If a page could belong to any startup, it has drifted.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Three named presets × light/dark, all driven by the same semantic and component tokens.",
      "Each preset owns its type pairing, corner language and depth model.",
      "Springy, pokeable components with press-scale, nudging arrows and spring-in overlays.",
      "Fluid typography and spacing; no hard-coded values anywhere.",
      "Native HTML interactive components that work without JavaScript."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Kanto Rule",
        "body": "Every primitive hue is named after a Kanto town from Pokémon — Lavender, Saffron, Celadon, Cerulean, Viridian, Vermilion, Fuchsia. A new hue gets a new Kanto town name, never a generic `blue`/`green`.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Roles-Only Rule",
        "body": "Components read semantic or component tokens, never a primitive ramp step. The one sanctioned exception is a preset's own backdrop (the Opal aurora), which mixes primitives directly.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Override-Only Rule",
        "body": "A preset may only override tokens the default theme already defines; the token build fails otherwise (ADR 0003/0004).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Shared Scale Rule",
        "body": "Presets change *how* type looks, never *how big* it is. Size steps are global so layout is identical across worlds.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Eager Fonts Rule",
        "body": "Every preset's faces are self-hosted and loaded up front, only in the weights the roles use, so switching worlds never flashes a fallback.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Depth-Is-Preset Rule",
        "body": "Never add a shadow or blur in component CSS. Components read `--color-surface-shadow`, `--color-surface-shadow-tight` and `--color-surface-backdrop`; the preset decides whether those are `none`, glow or glass.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Reading Default Rule",
        "body": "Default is the landing look and the look for reading: someone arriving on an article should be able to just read. Default can become more distinctive, but never by adding distraction. No backdrop, ambient motion or decoration competes with the text; showier ideas belong in other themes.",
        "section": "overview"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep Default calm enough to read an article in; push distinctiveness into other themes (The Reading Default Rule).",
      "Do read every styled property from a token custom property. No literal values in component CSS (ADR 0002).",
      "Do let the preset decide radius, depth, backdrop and type family. Components consume `--surface-*`, `--button-*` and `--color-surface-*` roles.",
      "Do describe each preset by its own personality when extending it: Default warm and bookish, Blueprint strong, technical and precise, Opal polished but friendly.",
      "Do give every interactive component a physical response (press, nudge, spring) and a `prefers-reduced-motion` fallback.",
      "Do build interactive pieces on native elements (`<dialog>`, radio inputs) so they work without JavaScript (ADR 0009).",
      "Do name new primitive hues after Kanto towns."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add backdrops, ambient motion or decoration to Default.",
      "Don't drift into the generic SaaS/template look: centred hero, three feature cards, gradient call-to-action.",
      "Don't hard-code a shadow, blur or radius in a component; that breaks the Depth-Is-Preset Rule.",
      "Don't change type *sizes* per preset; only families, weights, tracking and case.",
      "Don't reference a primitive ramp step from a component (`--color-saffron-700`); use the semantic role.",
      "Don't add a preset token the default theme doesn't define; the build rejects it."
    ]
  }
}